---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import NavBar from "../components/NavBar.astro";

const installCommand =
  "codex plugin marketplace add dianedef/ShipGlows --ref main --sparse .agents/plugins --sparse plugins/shipglows";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Install ShipGlows for Codex"
  description="Add ShipGlows to Codex in a few minutes: marketplace command, plugin install path, and the first useful workflow command."
>
  <main class="page-shell">
    <NavBar />

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Install ShipGlows</p>
        <h1 class="page-title">Install ShipGlows once. Stop losing time on the first move.</h1>
        <p>
          ShipGlows is the shortest serious path from “I have work to do” to
          “the right workflow is actually running.” You install one Codex
          plugin named <code>shipglows</code>, add the marketplace source once,
          and start with one plain instruction instead of memorizing a long
          command catalog.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary" href="#install-steps">Install ShipGlows now</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/skills/shipglows">See what the router does</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="doc-grid">
        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>Who this is for</h3>
          <p>
            Founders, operators, and builders who want Codex to route real work
            more cleanly without starting every thread from scratch.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>What changes after install</h3>
          <p>
            You get one public entrypoint that can answer directly, choose the
            right ShipGlows workflow, or ask one bounded clarification when the
            route is genuinely ambiguous.
          </p>
          <p>
            You can also steer the AI with simple fast tags like
            <code>#offer</code>, <code>#clarity</code>, or
            <code>#shipglows</code> instead of rewriting the whole instruction.
          </p>
          <a class="doc-action" href="/docs#recommended-combos">Open the focus-tags cheatsheet</a>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>What this is not</h3>
          <p>
            Not a pile of disconnected prompts, and not a claim that the plugin
            already contains every private reference. It is the clean public
            starting layer.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="install-steps">
      <div class="doc-grid">
        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>1. Add the marketplace source</h3>
          <p>
            This tells Codex where the ShipGlows plugin marketplace lives in the
            public repository.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{installCommand}</code></pre>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>2. Restart Codex</h3>
          <p>
            Restart Codex so the new marketplace source is loaded into the
            plugin directory.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>3. Install the plugin</h3>
          <p>
            Open the plugin directory, choose the <code>ShipGlows</code>
            marketplace, then install the <code>shipglows</code> plugin.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>4. Start with one plain instruction</h3>
          <p>
            The fastest first-success command is:
          </p>
          <pre><code>$shipglows help me choose the right workflow</code></pre>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="cta-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">Need the Windows development runtime?</p>
        <h2>The Codex plugin and the local DevServer are separate installs.</h2>
        <p>
          This page installs the ShipGlows workflow plugin for Codex. If you
          also want to clone repositories, run local development servers, and
          prepare the complete Windows development environment for Flutter for
          web, Android, and Windows, use the separate ShipGlows runtime installer.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/shipglows">
            Open the Windows development installer
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="cta-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">First-run outcome</p>
        <h2>You do not need to decide between build, bug, deploy, content, or docs first.</h2>
        <p>
          The public plugin is intentionally lightweight, but it already gives
          you the part that matters first: a visible routing layer. ShipGlows
          can answer a simple question, choose the right workflow move, and
          keep the handoff visible in the main thread. When a workflow needs
          the fuller corpus, ShipGlows can route you there explicitly instead of
          pretending the plugin is something it is not.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary" href="/skills/shipglows">Open the shipglows skill page</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/skill-modes">Read the workflow cheatsheet</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="doc-grid">
        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>What happens in the first five minutes</h3>
          <p>
            Install the plugin, run one plain request, and let ShipGlows decide
            whether the next move is a build flow, bug loop, maintenance pass,
            release path, content workflow, design route, or a direct answer.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>If you need the full corpus</h3>
          <p>
            Some workflows still need the optional complete ShipGlows source tree.
            The plugin can route you there explicitly when that happens.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>If you want the pack roadmap</h3>
          <p>
            ShipGlows keeps one public plugin first. Internal packs remain
            packaging infrastructure until they are genuinely portable and ready
            for public installation.
          </p>
          <a class="doc-action" href="/docs">Open the docs overview</a>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>If you are still evaluating</h3>
          <p>
            Read the public skill page first. It explains what the `shipglows`
            entrypoint actually does before you commit to the wider framework.
          </p>
          <a class="doc-action" href="/skills/shipglows">Open the shipglows page</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
